Turnover is not just costly in talent — each teacher departure costs $7,000–$21,000 in recruitment, onboarding, and effectiveness ramp-up, creating a hidden fiscal argument for retention investment.
Teachers who stay 5+ years are dramatically more effective than first-year teachers — experience compounds; losing experienced teachers destroys this accumulated human capital.
Where salary increases have been implemented (DC school reform, Minnesota rural incentives), surveys show both applications and student outcomes improved, demonstrating the causal direction.
